This Pex test runs before the actual method execution and has the side effect of modifying the actual object. You can see it has all the inputs logged for exploration. Additionally, it is using Debug.Assert to confirm the conditions in the comment for certain properties that should maintain their respective values ie, the 'Count', 'IndexOf', 'LastIndexOf', etc.

However, in this case, you should keep in mind that the IndexOf and LastIndexOf results might be different:  

- "IndexOf" returns the index of the first occurrence of the item in the ArrayList.
- "LastIndexOf" returns the index of the last occurrence of the item in the ArrayList.

Also, note that ArrayList.Add method could return several values depending on the underlying type: 

- For List<T> it's always "Count" minus one.
- For ArrayList it could be the new position of the new item or -1 if the item couldn't be added (for instance if the ArrayList has been fulled).

Finally, keep in mind that by performing these tests on a real object (an ArrayList in this case), you could actually modify its state, which could cause further side effects or inconsistencies in other parts of your solution.

As always, the specific details may vary depending on the ArrayList API and the actual behavior of the tested code. Always consult the official documentation and test your code according to its specifications.
  
Note: If you just want to verify the correctness of the contract (i.e., you don't care about the specifics of adding items), it is possible to write a contract test that doesn't mutate the state (i.e., just tests that the returned value/side-effects of the method are as expected without actually modifying the list, or calling functions that mutate it). This is a very good practice in unit testing since it ensures that other tests won't depend on the specific implementation details of a single method. Always choose a balance between the need for exact guarantees and the cost of running such tests. 

Another note: Using partial testing and specifying assumptions might help in cases where ArrayList contract is defined in multiple classes or when adding item could be performed differently in different conditions eg, on full/empty ArrayList.
  
Source: https://github.com/Microsoft/vstest/blob/master/docs/analyze.md#partial-testing-of-code-under-test-using-code-coverage.md
 
