}

You may be able to replace these assertions with your own, or replace the Debug.Assert call with a proper error reporting mechanism. 
Regarding the preconditions, I used the AssumePrecondition class within the Pex/Playback framework to "Assume" that the method under test will be tested on a state where a condition is true. Please ensure that you have the correct precondition for the method you are testing. 

Also, make sure you are testing the right methods, as the naming can change based on your implementation within ArrayList class. This is a contract test and it's tested on the basis of a method's return, its side-effects, state of the object after its execution, behavior on invalid inputs.
 
Remember to run your tests multiple times, because fuzz test inputs can help to cover all possible invalid inputs or edge cases your tests didn't cover.

Hope this helps, if you have any issue or any other questions feel free to ask.


A: This error is caused by the input to the method being invalid. Usually, this is because the method was called with arguments that violate the method's preconditions.

In the case of ArrayList.Add method, its precondition is that the object being added must be of a type compatible with the element type of the ArrayList (the type specified when the ArrayList was created). If the preconditions are not met - for example if the type of the parameter Object to add is not compatible with the element type of this ArrayList - it throws InvalidCastException.

You might want to try calling the ArrayList.Add method with argument compatible with the element type of the ArrayList. Here's a sample code:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void TestArrayListAddString()
{
    ArrayList array = new ArrayList();

    // This string will pass precondition.
    string itemType1 = "Test String";
    array.Add(itemType1);

    // This string will raise an InvalidCastException.
    Byte itemType2 = 100;
    try
    {
        array.Add(itemType2);
    }
    catch (InvalidCastException e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("InvalidCastException thrown as expected.");
    }
}
```

In this test, the first Add operation will pass and the second Add operation will throw an InvalidCastException because the ArrayList's preconditions would be violated. It is the responsibility of the method's callers to ensure they adhere to the preconditions. In a sense, this is good practice and reduces the likelihood of runtime exceptions. This is why it is also important to have good unit test coverage, including tests that exercise various preconditions to ensure your code behaves as expected.

Hope this helps in understanding why this error is being thrown. If you are trying to test for a specific InvalidCastException scenario, please try to check the specific InvalidCastException exception details too - this would give you a more specific idea of what is causing the issue.

Here is the detailed exception message: 

```
InvalidCastException: Specified cast is not valid.
   at System.Collections.ArrayList.Add(Object value)
   at YourTestMethodHere()
```

In this message, "Specified cast is not valid--------------------        }
    }

}
``` 

#### PexObserve ####
PexObserve lets you observe the values of your variables and verify your precondition before execution and postcondition after execution. 

#### PexAssumeUnderTest ####
PexAssumeUnderTest is used to tell Pex to automatically test an application method even if it is in the System namespace.

In the sample provided, the purpose of this faked test method is to perform unit testing on an ArrayList by adding an item into it. This is one of the many strategies that can be taken in unit testing. 

Test method PUT_AddContract adds an integer value to an ArrayList using the Add() method. It uses PexAssumeUnderTest to let Pex know that this method under test is in testable form and that it is outside the System namespace which means an assertion error won't occur during Pex test. 

It adds the integer value to an ArrayList and then uses assert to test the added value. This can be used to test many other aspects that this class might have, such as removing items or checking the count of items in the list.

Before running the PexUnit test, make sure to set up the Pex settings and to run the initialize method. After the run completes, Pex will display the results of its analysis, giving you an overview of all the potential paths in the code that it has explored. This can show you that some code paths aren't being tested or may have flaws.

This is a very basic overview of Pex testing and can be expanded upon depending on the complexity of the class being tested and how much branching the code comprises. Remember, the purpose is to automatically test your code, thus making debugging process much easier. For further information, please refer to Pex and Moles documentation. 

To fully utilize these tools, a better understanding of the code being tested and the purpose of each method will be beneficial. Furthermore, comprehensive test automation (like those carried out in Pex) will only reveal the surface of the potential issues and may require manual intervention to verify other functionalities that are not tested adequately by automated tests. - Byron Mons (Microsoft DevDiv)


    
    

   
    


   
    
   
    
   
    
   
   

   
    


   
    
    
    
    
   
   
   

   
    
    


   
   


   
    


   
    

    


   
    

   
    
    
   
    
   
    
   
   

   
    
    
    
    
   
   
    
   
    
   
    
    
    
   
    
   
    
    
   
   
   

   
    


   
   


   
    


   
    

    


   
   


   
   


   
  

   
    


   
   
    
    


   
    
   


   
   
    


   
   
    


   
    


   
    

    


   
    

   
    
    
   
    
   
    
   
    
   
   
    
    
    

# Visual Studio Extension

To have an outline of the code:
- Use "Code Outline" window.

To have a quick information about an identitfier :
- Use "Quick Info"

To have a list of methods that can be applied to an object:
- Use "Call Hierarchy" window.

To refactor the naming of a function:
- Use "Rename" 

To automatically insert braces {}
- Use "Automate Work"

To find all the references of a method:
- Use "Find All References"

To generate Unit Test Class:
- Use "Generate Unit Tests".

To debug the code:
- Use "Debugging"  

To run and build your code:
- Use "Build"

To auto complete what you type:
- Use "IntelliSense"

To locate a bug or issue in the source code:
- Use "Structure" > "Find" > "Issues"

If you want to execute your unit testing within the Visual Studio IDE:
- Use "Testing" > "Run" > "All Tests"

